WEBVTT

00:00:34.648 --> 00:00:58.345
And I want to welcome you all to today's event about perception and experience. Some topics where I struggled a little bit to get like an overarching theme. So last event was Dreams. There you have a very good way to structure everything to see, okay, what is a dream? How did Jung approach dreams? What is interpretation and so on?

00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:23.679
I really tried to find some hook or overarching structure for this event and failed a little bit, I think, but everything is related to perception. Everything is related to experience, but it will be more like a collection of views of Jung. And Jung was a very famous Swiss psychoanalyst, psychologist, a professor.

00:01:23.872 --> 00:01:35.585
He taught, he wrote, he traveled the world to experience and analyze people. All over the world, traveled to India, to Africa, to America, and just learn a lot.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:51.751
This was really the time where the world became already connected and there were very different institutions that tried to gather the knowledge of the world around beginning of the 20th century.

00:01:52.023 --> 00:02:12.662
And they had Eranos that had annual meetings where people would present all the knowledge that they could find from ancient cultures and art and so on. And it was this huge boom of just knowledge about everything that human beings have been doing for hundreds, thousands of years.

00:02:13.407 --> 00:02:21.015
And Jung was right in the middle there as psychoanalyst who went his own way.

00:02:21.856 --> 00:02:45.931
To explore deeper the mysteries, let's say, of human experience and what human experience meant for people through the time and how they codified it in culture, in mythology, in religion, in storytelling and everything, and try to really connect everything.

00:02:46.222 --> 00:03:04.785
And his way of connecting it, or rather to finding out that there were a lot of connections that he did not think about before, is through psychology and the psyche, because there is what the basis of everything, which is there for every human being.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:29.289
And he looked at it, how humans experience things, because as a psychoanalyst, He had a lot to do with schizophrenics and people who were psychotic, people who were neurotic and paranoid and so on. And they had a very special view on things.

00:03:29.886 --> 00:03:46.195
And he noticed that this view did not differ so much from the experience that people, for example, have when they dream or how things are codified in mythology. And in religion, that there are certain themes that come up again and again.

00:03:46.293 --> 00:04:07.159
And this laid the foundation for his works into the collective parts of human psyche, of the human nature, which is same for all the human beings and is like an underground connection to everyone. And where you are connected to basically every human being that ever existed.

00:04:07.908 --> 00:04:26.094
Human beings thousands or tens of thousands of years ago, and even to animals. And he begins his psychological view of human experience really with the center of experience, which is the ego.

00:04:26.807 --> 00:04:47.312
And the best way to describe the ego is you, you sitting here now and experiencing this presentation. And being aware of it, this is the ego and the ego function, where just it's a spotlight in the dark that's looking around and illuminating the world and becoming aware of the world.

00:04:48.402 --> 00:05:10.343
And around it, consciousness forms like a web, and everything that's experienced gets connected to this web of consciousness. So when you, for example, learn a new language, The experience of the new language is rather strange because it's just sounds and you can't make heads or tails of it.

00:05:10.937 --> 00:05:30.025
So for the last years, I learned Russian and the experience of learning a language is you have the sounds and you can't really understand words, sentences. You don't get the meanings. You don't even understand where one when a word begins and when it ends.

00:05:31.326 --> 00:05:40.814
So it is through learning the language that you're then able to understand, ah, these sounds mean this and so on.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:06:04.207
And with time, it's able to realize that there are pauses between words that you normally don't recognize when you don't know the language so well, but then there comes this fine-grained adjustment to it, to the sounds. To make a categorization and realize, okay, now you have words, you have sentences, you have meaning.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:29.087
And this slow uncovering process is— the language is already there, the people are saying something, but you cannot really process this in your mind. And this is a conscious function for Jung, to be able to integrate these foreign parts, for example, from a language into your own consciousness, to be able to understand the language, speak the language, act with it willfully.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:55.083
But not only the willfully part, to speak, for example, but really this perception part. And the reason that Carl Gustav Jung saw for consciousness, and which makes human beings a little bit unique, of having consciousness, is that Consciousness for Jung is mitigator. It's a problem solver.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:07:20.111
So the reason for consciousness is to solve problem and for the ego to spring into action and to do something. And everybody knows this, that there's, let's say, two very different modes of being. One is quite automatic. For example, when you brush your teeth or when you drive a car or when you go to work. The body does it all by itself.

00:07:20.921 --> 00:07:36.868
So you're so used to it and you have these patterns down so much that you don't have really to think about it. This is especially prevalent when you speak a new language. At a certain point, you don't have to think about speaking a new language, or when you play an instrument, you don't have to think about playing the instrument at a certain point.

00:07:38.236 --> 00:07:51.363
And this is the automatic mode. But then there's a more conscious attention mode. For example, when you make a mistake in a language and you have to think about it, or when something unforeseen happens.

00:07:51.459 --> 00:08:12.257
For example, you drive your car to work as you have done it hundreds of thousands of times, and suddenly your motor acts up or a tire blows, and suddenly you're 100% aware what's happening. There's a very good book by Daniel Kahneman, Thinking, Fast and Slow, and he writes about these two systems, System 1 and System 2.

00:08:12.627 --> 00:08:32.187
Which has very different jobs in acting and in experiencing by having this very automatic, fast responses, automatic motions, automatic reactions, and so on. And then this very deliberate and slow and very taxing mode of being.

00:08:32.706 --> 00:08:52.225
When you're learning something new and you have to concentrate, you can't keep up this level of consciousness and awareness for a very long time. So it's necessary to be aware. So when you learn a new language, you really have to concentrate on the new words, grammar, and all those stuff and listen very closely.

00:08:53.218 --> 00:09:09.369
And this is System 1, that's System 2, where you have to pay a lot of attention to bring it into this automatic mode. So if you do it often enough, then it becomes automatic and you don't have to think about it.

00:09:09.626 --> 00:09:32.251
When you get more professional in the language, you can listen to it longer, you can read in it longer than 5 minutes, 10 minutes, maybe for hours even. And this is then automatically. So, but the work of consciousness is to bring together very different demands.

00:09:33.176 --> 00:09:51.153
So you have You have your internal desires, your needs, your things you have to do, you want to do. And of course, you have the external circumstances that also want things from you and that pose certain limitations that you then try to mitigate.

00:09:51.919 --> 00:10:12.654
I don't know how many of you have seen Rick and Morty, but I think in the first season, there's this figure of Mr. Meeseeks who gets spawned and is living just for a specific task. And for example, Meeseeks has to grab a bottle of water and then he brings back the bottle of water and disappears. And consciousness is like that.

00:10:13.248 --> 00:10:33.971
So there's a problem, it needs to be solved, and it's in a very active state. And as soon as the work is done, consciousness can turn back because being conscious all the time is incredibly taxing. People have to sleep 8 hours a day.

00:10:34.212 --> 00:10:45.903
And when you work very hard mentally, you're also tired and you have to lie down and do something else, turn off your mind, watch TV, whatever, just to recharge this consciousness again.

00:10:47.140 --> 00:11:10.480
So consciousness tries to do novel, complicated, new stuff to integrate it into the psyche of the person to learn something, to be better. And Jung had this great quote that I found, and he said, nobody will ever become conscious if he does not hit his head on something.

00:11:11.874 --> 00:11:33.252
And this is what therapy is very often about, to hit people onto something and let them become aware of something that they tried to turn away from, where they tried not to be aware of. And to get this mode of having new information integrated into the psyche.

00:11:34.608 --> 00:11:53.871
So we have our consciousness, it's now active, it's supposed to do something, it has a job, and we don't have so much time because it's getting tired very easily. And part of this automatic process that's running in the background is the psychic background of the psyche.

00:11:54.032 --> 00:12:17.289
And this is the part that is always a question, whether it's cultural or natural, how much is given from human nature, how much is shaped through outside, whether it's coming from outside or inside. And we have so many perceptive function and experience function that automatically that we don't really think about it.

00:12:19.285 --> 00:12:37.222
That we cannot really say whether we objectively see the world, or rather that we see it in a subjective manner. And we think it's objective because everybody sees the same way. The example that Jung had was we don't really see wavelengths, we see colors. So wavelengths are there.

00:12:37.463 --> 00:13:01.967
There are electromagnetic wavelengths that are everywhere, and you have a huge spectrum, a huge spectrum that goes from microwaves, when you have a microwave, or nuclear radiation, or cell towers and all of this, but you have a very small spectrum, roughly from 300 to 700 nanometers, where you see colors.

00:13:03.449 --> 00:13:28.052
And if it's within the spectrum, you can see it. If it's not, it's outside. You have certain animals who are night predators who can see rather more into, I think it was the infrared part. And their eyes and their psyche is more accustomed to see this, to have the ability to see this. But we don't really see the wavelengths with the numbers. We see colors.

00:13:28.313 --> 00:13:51.290
And this is an automatic process. And this is something that is again unique for human beings to see colors because many animals in the world, they can't see colors. For example, dogs, black and white, many animals not able to see colors. So humans have very great vision. Same with music or audio in general.

00:13:51.965 --> 00:14:16.669
You have wavelengths in the air, which is just atoms moving, and the brain automatically generates out of that music sounds. Foreign language that you're trying to learn at the moment. So it is already something happening automatically that is similar to this example from the fish swimming in the water and not being aware of the water because the water's always there.

00:14:16.717 --> 00:14:29.307
We always see color normally. We can always hear normally. So it's just a given and a foundation for our experiences. But when we now see something and when we hear something.

00:14:30.353 --> 00:14:52.348
It goes for Jung farther than this in the sense that we are only being able to aware and be processed of things that can latch on onto consciousness. This is again the example of language. When you have a foreign language that you don't know, you don't know what it says. You can't really distinguish the words or the sentences.

00:14:53.095 --> 00:15:10.187
There's nothing in consciousness there in the psyche. Where this could be processed correctly. So it goes right through. And this filtering, of course, is not only for languages, but rather to understanding concepts in general.

00:15:10.251 --> 00:15:29.772
And this makes it very hard when you learn something new, for example, Jungian psychology, to get a grip on it because everything is suddenly strange and foreign. You maybe have ideas how it could be, but only through time and enough examples, you'll be able to process that. And this is a filtering that's taking place.

00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:53.084
And additionally to that, you have consciousness, which is small, limited, has a task, and is not able to keep up for a very long time. And it's focused on very, very specific things. So it's similar to the eye where the area you can really see is pretty much as big as your thumbnail.

00:15:53.196 --> 00:16:15.450
When you stretch out the arm and raise up the thumb and look at your thumb, the part of the eye that can see very clearly is roughly the size of your thumbnail. And everything else, the brain basically makes up. There's some rough shapes and so on, and the rest gets basically interpreted by the brain automatically.

00:16:15.934 --> 00:16:33.031
But because you're scanning around with your eyes and everywhere where you look directly, you have this very fine view on things that's high resolution. You think, ah, everything is high resolution. Yeah, everything is high resolution if you look at it, if you look everywhere.

00:16:34.348 --> 00:16:50.127
And this is part of consciousness and this is a downside of consciousness. This extreme limitation, but it's like a very small hole where the whole world has to fit through and where the world is channeled through.

00:16:53.678 --> 00:17:15.423
So Jung saw not really that people were seeing and experiencing the objective world, that they're just objects outside and we see them really as they are, but rather we have a perception of them. And this was not really for him a downside. He did not say just because it's subjective, does it mean that it's not real?

00:17:16.407 --> 00:17:36.364
Because that's a normal expectation that people have. So, okay, yeah, I see the objective world, I see the material stuff, and this is what is real. This is a very materialistic view. Even subjective experience can be real. Even when one is alone, but experiencing it, especially everything inside the psyche.

00:17:38.117 --> 00:18:02.599
And he said, real is what works. Real is what has an effect on people. When somebody has a neurosis or schizophrenic breakdown, it does not bring something to tell the person, yeah, that there are no voices here in the room. No, the things you are seeing or you're imagining are not real because they're not physically here.

00:18:04.377 --> 00:18:26.156
They are real because a person is experiencing them. It has an effect on the person, these experiences and the things that they're perceiving. So they have to be taken seriously. And here Jung was on the bleeding edge, let's say, of psychology. Psychology back then was a very new science.

00:18:27.246 --> 00:18:48.956
And in the past it was said when people would have mental problems, that they would have a damaged brain, which is a materialistic view, right? You have the brain, it's damaged, it can't work properly. So what you do, you take the people, lock them away. And people were locked away in very horrible conditions because they were damaged goods. Nothing you could do.

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:58.061
You just wait till they die. And Carl Gustav Jung said the conditions, they were so horrible that even normal people would go crazy. What they need to live in there.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:22.151
But this was around the time when psychoanalysis came up with Freud, and there was a view of it's not that the brain is damaged, but rather the people have a psychological problem, like a knot inside their psyche that needs to be solved and can be solved through talking. Jung, Freud established this idea of the talking therapy.

00:19:22.634 --> 00:19:44.146
That you can talk with people through their problems, through their experiences and their perceptions. Jung said one of the reasons what made psychotic episodes and schizophrenic episodes so dangerous was that people thought that they were going crazy.

00:19:44.696 --> 00:19:56.632
So they would perceive something, experience something that was so out of the ordinary that they said to themselves, I can't really tell anyone about this because people would not understand. They would say I would be crazy.

00:19:57.786 --> 00:20:15.276
And people would go then into, this was Jung's view, into a kind of isolation where they would try to remove themselves from family, friends, society, and so on, which is rather enforcing these problems.

00:20:16.206 --> 00:20:31.862
An unsolved problem, like a trauma in the back of the mind, something that could not be processed, something where consciousness realized it cannot process this. Same as you would eat something and you would vomit and give up there. The body knows what it can process and what it cannot process.

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:45.061
Even the tongue is already able to know before it's in the stomach whether something is good or not. For example, poisons are very bitter. And the taste of bitterness is especially to find out about poisons.

00:20:46.250 --> 00:21:05.445
And this is why small children especially are incredibly sensible to bitterness and bitter taste and dislike them and don't want to eat anything bitter because it would be harmful for the child in the development. And the psyche can do basically the same according to Jung, realize whether it can process something or not.

00:21:08.977 --> 00:21:28.707
And here he would then sit down with the patients and let them talk and let them know that it's understood and something normal is happening to them. And he had even one patient who had crazy visions about burning wheels and everything.

00:21:28.964 --> 00:21:51.181
And he said he's afraid to talk to anyone and nobody would understand and so on. He felt like he's the only person who had ever had this in whole of humanity. And Jung said, let me grab a book. And he reached into his shelf, grabbed the 400-year-old alchemic treatise and showed him the page with a picture and said, you're not alone. You're not the first one to have this.

00:21:51.261 --> 00:22:14.033
Somebody else had it 400 years ago. And this would give people already the strength and understanding that they're not alone and that they could reconnect, that they're not outside of humanity. That they have not experienced something that would mark them as an outcast, but rather there's just an unusual experience, which is not unusual if taking enough people and enough time.

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:38.562
But this is an idea of real is what works. When people have a vision and an experience and it has a lasting impression on them, it means it's real to them. Feelings are real. Thoughts are real. As soon as they influence how we think, how we behave, and so on, It's the same as when we walk against a tree or a car drives into us.

00:22:38.643 --> 00:22:51.573
It's the same realness, but from another perspective, not from the outside, but from the inside. So it is this felt experience.

00:22:54.280 --> 00:23:12.873
What Carl Gustav Jung would try to say to unify this that we have this outside world and we have this inside world where the rules are different but similar in the way how they are processed. You would say that the mind, psyche would codify this in images.

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:30.814
So everything we do, that we touch and that we get into contact with turns into a psychic image and into a psychic content. And we rather react and interact with the psychic content than with the outside thing.

00:23:30.991 --> 00:23:50.771
We might think that the outside thing is what we think it is, but we are more or less interacting with the psychic content that's then in the psyche. Example, when you see a murder mystery movie, you see it and you think, ah, this is the killer.

00:23:51.412 --> 00:24:02.597
And the movie structures in a way to bring you to this conclusion, but then it comes out, ah, something else happened entirely. So the brain and the mind constantly tries to predict what something is.

00:24:03.126 --> 00:24:23.545
Of course, often has to do with very little information and rather take something that it knows and that it already has in its repertoire and its consciousness and use it and say, okay, yeah, this fits. This is like a solution that works so long as until the point where it does not work anymore.

00:24:23.755 --> 00:24:29.890
And then consciousness has to come back and say, okay, what I know did not fit. Now I have to find something new.

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:52.580
This is especially true when you, for example, do troubleshooting and you have a problem with your computer, your car, whatever at work, and you think you know what the problem is, and then you find out, oh no, I had no idea what the problem is and I have to think about what really the problem is. Finding out the problem is already half of the work, if not even more.

00:24:53.802 --> 00:25:12.543
So it does not matter really if it comes from outside or from inside. It becomes a psychic content image as soon as we are in contact with it. So there's this labeling taking place that something is outside or inside, but everything gets mingled together. In the mind. So everything gets into one pot.

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:36.682
And here Jung sees, and we are basically looking at the pot, our ego, our consciousness looking in the pot and see, okay, well, what's there? And here Jung tries to explain how there can be psychological experiences that are not based on the outside world.

00:25:37.010 --> 00:26:00.601
How you can, for example, see, experience a ghost or encounter God or an angel or the devil or demons or whatever. To have these experiences in there and they feel real. And even when you close your eyes, very likely you have images that just pop up.

00:26:00.892 --> 00:26:19.263
Or when you dream, you also have images that pop up and that can feel real in the sense that they have an effect on you. And when you have a nightmare, you don't question the reality of the nightmare because it happened to you. You experienced it even though that outside nothing happened.

00:26:21.110 --> 00:26:43.572
So you have the images, you have inside and outside, and Jung goes even further to say we don't see the objective world. We see something very subjective, like a subjective interpretation of the world that we are not really aware of because we have biases, we use heuristics and everything.

00:26:44.808 --> 00:27:04.557
He did not say that, but he alluded to it. That would be the, the modern terms for it, that the psyche has to use certain shortcuts to basically make sense of the world because the world is endless and it's big and limitless. And we only have a very small brain in comparison, a very limited brain.

00:27:05.583 --> 00:27:19.899
And you can see this also in optical illusions that our visual cortex is using certain tricks to get a result which can be then bypassed or misused.

00:27:20.476 --> 00:27:43.916
To create an optical illusion and give the impression that something looks, for example, 3D, even though that's not really 3D for real, at least not in front of one objectively 3D. So we see this pre-filtered content. We have several layers of filtering happening.

00:27:44.271 --> 00:28:05.981
We have something, it becomes an Image that is then we are able to perceive it or not, whether it's already part of our consciousness, whether it can dock somewhere. This is why it's so been so boring in school so often because a lot of information, there's just no access point to dock it.

00:28:07.002 --> 00:28:26.084
And the more you learn about something and the more you know about something, the more you can process new information. The more fun it is. Most fun is to do things that you can do already well and you can develop there. This is a flow state. Again, it's not something that Jung has been talking about directly.

00:28:26.149 --> 00:28:44.833
He did not call it flow state, but to have this conscious development going on. And then of course you have your personality that's filtering additionally how you see the world. And he had a personality structure.

00:28:46.198 --> 00:29:09.146
He called it a typology where he said we have certain cognitive functions or more perceiving functions. His, his model of personality was very much based on perception and experiencing things. So the four functions were sensation, Thinking, feeling, and intuition.

00:29:09.981 --> 00:29:26.920
Sensation, meaning to be experiencing something is there. So, ah, there is something outside. There's a tree inside. There's an emotion. Then thinking is a labeling. So I already said it, the outside, there's something, it's a tree labeling. Ah, it's rage when it's an emotion.

00:29:27.707 --> 00:29:51.866
And feeling, doing like a value judgment, whether You are happy that the tree is there, or you're not happy that the tree is there. For example, when you drove a car and there's suddenly a tree where you don't expect it, maybe you're happy about the rage for what reason ever. You're at a sports game or something.

00:29:53.350 --> 00:30:03.881
And then intuition to basically perceive something through the unconscious, which is not directly there, but rather possibility that something could be in the future. It's more like a time function.

00:30:04.716 --> 00:30:25.075
So you see there, there are different channels where information can come into the psyche and to the ego and to consciousness where he based his typology around to say, okay, there are certain venues that people like more. Thinking people like thinking a lot and sensation people like sensating a lot.

00:30:25.721 --> 00:30:49.929
And those people live different ways because they like to do it that way. He said, for example, sensation people are very likely stockbrokers. They see the numbers and things that are happening there. So they are really just what is there now in this moment. And intuitive people are more artistic and artists. They rather see possibilities.

00:30:50.479 --> 00:31:03.239
For example, gamblers. So gamblers have to believe that there's a win to keep on gambling. Or hunters, he said. So people who try to perceive things that are not really there, but who really like to do it that way.

00:31:07.189 --> 00:31:22.051
And it is because there's this kind of favorite channel that the focus on perceiving the world is on that, which is another limitation. There are many different ways to do it, but you have a favorite one.

00:31:22.148 --> 00:31:38.325
And it's just, he said that everybody comes into the world with a favorite function that they like to use the most and most likely build a career around because they are most capable. It's like when you have a strong hand and a weak hand, you rather use the strong hand all the time.

00:31:40.777 --> 00:32:04.957
And it is this additional layer of filtering that's happening so that you Actually removing more and more from what we would call objective, all the things that are happening outside and they are graspable, that they get filtered and disturbed through different ways. And we are then focused with our consciousness on only small things, very likely in our favorite function.

00:32:05.006 --> 00:32:26.158
This is only one of four. And additionally, He said this labeling of inside and outside is not really an easy feat to say, okay, I'm touched by something. And of course it can come from inside and come from outside and the labeling takes place.

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:33.316
But that does not mean that we can really separate what is outside really happening and what is inside happening.

00:32:34.150 --> 00:32:56.922
And he said, so this is one very specific state, which was way more common back then, where consciousness was not strong, because consciousness has this separating, discriminating function of being very able to just separate things from another.

00:32:59.300 --> 00:33:27.742
He said the reason why, for example, in antiquity and in hunter-gatherer societies, people had very strong feelings about rivers, trees, stones, and so on, and said, oh, there's a god living in the river, and there's a nymph living in the tree, and so on, was that they were thinking they're perceiving the outside world, but rather it is from the inside world.

00:33:27.871 --> 00:33:47.357
They were looking into the pot where all the images come together. And did like a mislabeling, let's say, the separation from the outside world and the inside world, inside world being the psyche, is not really very accurate, let's say.

00:33:50.020 --> 00:34:14.853
This phenomenon he called participation mystique, wasn't his term, but rather from his teacher when he was in Paris. And it's this spreading out of the psychology that people think what they're experiencing outside is outside, but rather it is something from their own psychology. Example: astrology.

00:34:15.272 --> 00:34:37.128
When we look into the night sky and we see the stars, it's, if we want to be very unromantic, just bright dots, which very strangely distributed all over the night sky, and they have different intensity and so on. And what human beings saw there were constellations.

00:34:37.386 --> 00:35:01.929
They saw the fishes, and they saw a ram, and they saw scales, and the Scorpion, and so on. And they saw whole stories, and they saw gods. Those, let's say, human inventions, but humans did not think that they invented something, but rather that they perceived something. Jung said they perceived their own psychology.

00:35:03.810 --> 00:35:14.887
They saw the things and how the psyche was working and saw it outside and recorded it. And those are the things he was very interested in, in this projected psychology, how people saw things.

00:35:15.593 --> 00:35:34.557
Because this very often would tell him, okay, what is happening inside, just through people saying what they see, what they perceive, to see what the inside of the head looks like and what they actually see. And he had this example of painters.

00:35:34.896 --> 00:35:56.564
You can have 10 painters and put them in the same spot and tell them, okay, paint, paint what you see. You get different paintings, which is not only based on the capability of the people, how well they can paint, but rather also where their attentional focus lies at.

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:21.275
And he calls this Weltanschauung, so worldview, that people look into the world and they have a certain expectation how it is and how it works based on their own psychology. So people, he would say, had a very specific focus, same as a favorite function.

00:36:21.982 --> 00:36:43.556
People would have a perspective rather if they like the outside world or the inside world. The outside world, he would say, there was— would be strong empiricists And they would go through the world and try to experience it in all its individuality. And they would be more outgoing. He called them extraverted.

00:36:44.900 --> 00:37:00.912
And he coined the term and he coined also the other term introversion. So the people who are rather looking inside and try to see the world rather through concepts and mental constructs and ideas.

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:22.777
So they're not really looking outside in the world directly, but rather, rather looking in their own psyche to see rather a reflection of the real world inside themselves. That's the way how people prefer to experience the world when they're introverted. And this worldview, again, is something that people are not really aware of.

00:37:22.873 --> 00:37:46.231
You become aware of it when you, for example, talk with other people and talk about what you see. Term for personality psychology, so psychology about personality and personal differences, is differential psychology. And it only talks about the differences between people. So everything where people are different, there you can specify, there you can talk about.

00:37:46.809 --> 00:38:07.167
But if people are all the same, it makes it hard to talk about something. And to have these differences between people. And this Weltanschauung, worldview, he said, is of course very strong when you have artistic people or when you have philosophers.

00:38:07.996 --> 00:38:18.871
And he would say that about a lot of philosophers, that they made a confession about their own psychology by saying how they see the world.

00:38:19.336 --> 00:38:43.646
He talked a lot about Hegel and said Hegel was not for him a real philosopher, not like Schopenhauer, for example, but rather somebody who had a very strong worldview and talked about it and was not aware that Hegel was not experiencing the world as it is, but rather his own psychology projected in the world by looking into the pot, seeing those images and label them.

00:38:45.109 --> 00:39:05.316
Not in the correct way. But this is easier said than done because knowing really what is outside and what is inside is a strong feat. So additionally to all those layers I talked about, there's a layer he called attitude.

00:39:07.571 --> 00:39:20.836
And this is really how people would be prepared, what their expectations would be of the people, how they think about things.

00:39:21.192 --> 00:39:45.044
And he would talk mainly in the context of the unconscious, because the unconscious is everything that the consciousness is not, but it is automatic functions happening in the background and interacting with consciousness. For example, when you dream, the unconscious pushes up images and voices, sounds that the ego then experiences.

00:39:49.096 --> 00:40:01.723
And based on the strangeness of the contents and the view that people would have of the unconscious, the images would change. The perception and experience would be very different.

00:40:02.356 --> 00:40:18.678
So he noted, for example, that Extroverted people, people who are outside and perceive the outside, rather don't really believe in these automatic unconscious psychological phenomena that are happening in the background.

00:40:21.898 --> 00:40:56.670
And being able to be aware of that and expect that and being able to process that, which also plays in this idea of the Schizophrenics and psychotic people, or people who have these episodes, not being able to integrate that into their consciousness, not able to process it because there's nothing to dock there, is this idea of consciousness not being able to handle this and thus having a bad attitude towards, for example, unconscious contents because suddenly things are moving that should not move.

00:40:56.960 --> 00:41:12.996
When you're alone at home, And suddenly things start moving. Of course you're afraid because you cannot really categorize what is happening. So people would say that a ghost or like a foreign presence there.

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:26.298
And Jung said that's in relationship to the unconscious, very important to be aware that there's something as the unconscious, that it is working autonomously. But also that it's part of oneself.

00:41:27.245 --> 00:41:40.347
That is a little bit like an accident that certain things are separated from consciousness because again, it's filtered in such a way that we only have a small view of the world, to be honest.

00:41:41.712 --> 00:42:03.324
So if we only, let's say, live passively and let things rain down on us, The things, the consciousness, the worldview that we have is rather limited. And it is this active part, this aware part and open part that makes it possible to have this internal view of the world grow.

00:42:04.780 --> 00:42:22.021
He has said the attitude part is very important. What is the attitude towards things? And this is happening in therapy, he said, when he talks to people. To set things in a correct frame, to give it a correct context, to make it understandable.

00:42:22.361 --> 00:42:54.607
Same as showing this alchemical treatise to his patient who had this very weird experience, but also to talk with people about the things they don't want to talk about, personal problems, uh, trauma, and to be not only— and this is this complicated thing to explain— it is through the attitude that determines how the unconscious looks like.

00:42:56.930 --> 00:43:10.905
So the unconscious for Jung is threatening the more the consciousness tries to get away from it. So It becomes bigger, more frightening, the more you try to turn away from it.

00:43:12.460 --> 00:43:32.664
It's a little bit like in the movie Jaws, where the events get worse and worse and worse and worse because Brody is not— the protagonist is not approaching the shark, but rather running away from the shark because there's this relationship between how you act and how you perceive the things.

00:43:33.244 --> 00:43:53.597
So I hope I could explain this attitude concept well enough. So we have our consciousness that is acting and is perceiving very filtered, very incomplete information, but has a possibility to work in all those different ways. When we're focused on the outside, we can also choose to pay attention to the inside.

00:43:53.983 --> 00:44:11.387
When we have our favorite function, there's also the possibility to actively learn about the other ways of perceiving the world and to be aware that not everything that we experience outside has to be really outside, but rather there's also a labeling process happening.

00:44:13.015 --> 00:44:31.698
That's happening through us and can happen automatically, but it can also happen willfully and then be integrated so that this attitude, the correct attitude of context and reframing for perceptions and experience come, that they can be used in a useful manner, a process in the psyche. Of the people.

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:55.534
And this active approach Jung saw in consciousness and in ego. And the problem that there is for Jung in an unconscious state, nothing really has to make sense. Everything can be completely intertangled, intertwined, and undifferentiated, unsophisticated.

00:44:56.769 --> 00:45:20.624
But as soon as you focus on a certain part, you get it outside of the unconscious. And you only focus, again, this very small thumbnail part of your awareness. If smokers can focus very clearly on small parts of reality, but very often only on that part.

00:45:22.074 --> 00:45:35.085
And he says we are separating things by being conscious of something and paying attention to something because you are only focusing on a small part and have to ignore everything else.

00:45:35.650 --> 00:45:54.006
When you drive a car, you try to concentrate on the road and you try to ignore everything around you, the landscape, even how your back feels. And only after hours and hours, your back says like, okay, please make a pause, make a stretch.

00:45:56.620 --> 00:46:19.237
And by focusing on one thing, the other thing automatically gets discounted. So when you only focus on the outside, you ignore the inside because consciousness, limited, small, tires easily, can only focus on a small and when it's only in the outside, the inside, the internal world gets ignored.

00:46:20.715 --> 00:46:37.333
So you have this automatic separation happening as soon as you do something and become more aware of something. And in the unconscious, the counterpart automatically gets constellated.

00:46:38.682 --> 00:46:55.823
So A little bit in school, for example, primary school, when one would play with watercolors, you have all those different colors and then you start to mix them, they all become brown in the end.

00:46:56.586 --> 00:47:11.888
So if you try to imagine this process in reverse and you would start pulling out colors, you would have brown, this intertangled mess. Right? But you cannot really do something with it unless you want to have the color brown. But brown is also a little bit boring.

00:47:12.421 --> 00:47:35.085
If you want to have bright yellow, you have to think about that consciousness is removing this yellow part out of the brown part and then being able to use yellow as a color, being separated from the rest. A consciousness that's well developed can really take out all the colors again out of the brown mass.

00:47:35.786 --> 00:48:00.729
And make use of them all so that there's green and blue and orange and so on. And this is the way how Jung saw fundamentally how people perceive things by differences, by opposites. And here you saw also the way why people do something, because there's something not right.

00:48:00.958 --> 00:48:20.532
A little bit like electricity. You have a positive and you have the negative. Only if there's a difference in load, electrical load, then you have things happening between them. So a, a battery has uneven load in the sense that there's an equilibrium that's trying to return to this equilibrium state.

00:48:22.513 --> 00:48:45.084
And it's this separation part that generates energy, that generates things happening Because suddenly the unconscious becomes very aware to remind consciousness of there are all those other colors. That's not only yellow. You have to also think about all the other things. And Jung saw this codified in a lot of symbology worldwide.

00:48:45.581 --> 00:49:03.505
For example, in yin and yang, the, the Tao symbol where you have the white snake with the black eye and the black snake with the white eye, that you have separation of both and they both have to be in a sense separated to be perceived, but they in the end have to be unified in a new manner.

00:49:04.250 --> 00:49:17.752
So you have to take out all the colors and return them, let's say, to a place where every color can exist from itself, but still be part of the same set to be made use of.

00:49:18.543 --> 00:49:37.169
And it is The work of consciousness and the life's work that one has to make all those unconscious contents conscious and integrate them into consciousness.

00:49:37.522 --> 00:49:52.113
And this is for Jung individuation, to take this whole mess, to have this brown soup and extract all the colors again, because the colors are prettier than only the brown mass and the brown liquid.

00:49:54.802 --> 00:50:18.265
And here Jung, see, tried to explain where the difficulties lie, even on a perceptual level, how we see the world, that it's not to say, okay, what I see is real, what I experience is, it is real because it's happening to me, but, and does not mean that I automatically understand what I'm perceiving, what I'm experiencing.

00:50:19.035 --> 00:50:42.495
And that this, um, this conscious investigation into the experience, into the perception that makes it possible to create a well-formed human being and human being that is able to solve problems, to live a life, to live a fulfilling life. And he saw that really, that there's a function in the psyche that is telling people that they're living a fulfilling life.

00:50:42.896 --> 00:51:00.622
That they are on the right track. So there's always in the background something that's reminding people whether they're seeing the world and experiencing the world correctly. Because if they don't do it, the unconscious always pushes and tries to steer the person back on the right path.

00:51:01.361 --> 00:51:39.798
And it goes even so far when somebody is an intuitive And he, oh, what was it, sensation, let's say an intuitive, and the person is supposed to think because the thinking is not very well developed, then the psyche and the body would cause the person to have an accident or to get sick just to stay at one place and not be all around those possibilities to, but rather to think about this now.

00:51:39.910 --> 00:52:01.447
So the unconscious and the psyche tries to make people go there and do that, what they most likely don't want to do, don't like to see, and don't like to experience, to say like, hey, there's a whole big world and you only have a very small part of it that you have seen and that you have understood.

00:52:02.024 --> 00:52:23.512
So there's a lot of work more to do for you. And this would be my conclusion to the topic of perception and experience. And I hope it was structured well enough so that you could follow and learn something and also have maybe a little bit fun.

